          light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~81 km as low-confidence.
          Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin,
          volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
    3  Y   first_yield              3.306    57.09   7.900e+07  physical
          Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.1 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and
          heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5
          / H_f in [R22]).
    4  F   flare                    3.421    55.60   2.360e+08  stylized
          Light rises x17.2 in 0.29 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated
          with: yield gen 0 at 57.1 km (100% of m0); split gen 0 at 54.3 km (100% of m0). Dust groups
          supply 75% of the rise and 71% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation
          flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic
          cascade.
    5  B   first_breakup            3.519    54.33   5.144e+08  stylized
          Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as
          dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of
          a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification.
          Light grew x6.5 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
